## 1. All Nodes Distance K in Binary Tree (LeetCode 863)

**Concepts:** BFS, Parent Mapping, HashMap, HashSet

### Approach
- Build a parent map for each tree node.
- Starting from the target node, perform BFS.
- Explore left child, right child and parent.
- Use a visited set to avoid visiting nodes repeatedly.
- Collect nodes at distance `k`.

### Java Solution

```java
class Solution {
    public List<Integer> distanceK(TreeNode root, TreeNode target, int k) {
        Map<TreeNode, TreeNode> parent = new HashMap<>();
        Queue<TreeNode> q = new LinkedList<>();
        q.offer(root);

        while (!q.isEmpty()) {
            TreeNode node = q.poll();

            if (node.left != null) {
                parent.put(node.left, node);
                q.offer(node.left);
            }

            if (node.right != null) {
                parent.put(node.right, node);
                q.offer(node.right);
            }
        }

        Queue<TreeNode> bfs = new LinkedList<>();
        Set<TreeNode> visited = new HashSet<>();
        bfs.offer(target);
        visited.add(target);

        int distance = 0;

        while (!bfs.isEmpty()) {
            if (distance == k) {
                List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
                for (TreeNode node : bfs) {
                    result.add(node.val);
                }
                return result;
            }

            int size = bfs.size();

            for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
                TreeNode node = bfs.poll();

                TreeNode[] neighbors = {
                    node.left, node.right, parent.get(node)
                };

                for (TreeNode next : neighbors) {
                    if (next != null && visited.add(next)) {
                        bfs.offer(next);
                    }
                }
            }

            distance++;
        }

        return new ArrayList<>();
    }
}
```

**Time Complexity:** O(n)  
**Space Complexity:** O(n)

